### Transcation
--- A transaction is a sequence of one or more operations performed as a single logical unit of work. 
--- A transaction ensures that either all operations are completed successfully, or none of them take effect, maintaining the consistency and integrity of the database.

The Four Pillars: ACID Properties

Just like a strong building needs a solid foundation, transactions rely on ACID properties. Let's break these down:

1. Atomicity: All or nothing. Just like you want your full meal or no meal at all, a transaction must be fully completed or not done at all.
2. Consistency: The database always stays correct. If you add or remove dishes, your Swiggy cart should always show the right total.
3. Isolation: One transaction shouldn't interfere with another. Imagine you and a friend are both using the app to add dishes to the same order-you don't want their choices to mess up what you're adding!
4. Durability: Once it's done, it's done! After you confirm your Swiggy order, it's locked in-even if the internet cuts out, the order still shows.

Key Points to Remember

. Transaction = All or Nothing (Either all steps succeed or none are saved).
. Turn off auto-commit to manually control saving changes.
. Use commit() to save changes permanently.
. Use rollback() to undo all changes if something fails.
. Always turn auto-commit back on after finishing.
. Transactions ensure data consistency and reliability.
. Follow ACID properties (Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability).
Real-Life Applications of Transactions

Banking Systems
. Deduct money from one account and add it to another. Both steps must succeed together.

E-commerce Platforms (like Amazon, Swiggy, Zepto)
. Payment confirmation and order placement are tightly coupled.
Ticket Booking Apps
. Blocking a seat and processing payment are part of one transaction.
Inventory Management
. Updating stock when products are shipped and reflecting it in the database.
